INT. FIELD HOSPITAL - CONVERTED CHURCH - NIGHT

Pews pushed against the walls. Cots in the nave. An altar draped with a bloody sheet. A lone candle on the pulpit. Distant artillery, fading.

MAJOR SIOBHAN DELANEY-WRIGHT, 40s, surgeon, apron stiff with dried blood, writes in a ledger at a pew-desk. SERGEANT MIKHAIL TERZIC, 30s, bandaged arm, sits on a cot. NURSE ROSALIND PARK-EZEIGWE, 20s, hair pinned under a cap, adjusts an IV. PRIVATE OSWIN FALCONER, 18, sleeps in the next cot, face grey. Nearby, a radio operator, CORPORAL TEDDY AGUILAR, 20s, headphones around his neck, fiddles with a set.

SIOBHAN
Corporal. Anything?

TEDDY
Static, Major.

SIOBHAN
Try the other band.

He turns the dial. A voice comes through, clipped and tired.

RADIO (V.O.)
...say again, Dagger Six, this is Hammer. Enemy withdrawing along the river road. All units hold.

Siobhan looks up. So does Mikhail. Rosalind stills with the IV bag in her hand.

MIKHAIL
They're withdrawing?

TEDDY
That's what it says.

ROSALIND
Then it's over?

SIOBHAN
It's over tonight. Not tomorrow.

She closes the ledger. Stands. Wipes her palms on her apron. Crosses to Oswin's cot, checks his pulse. Nods. Draws the blanket up.

ROSALIND
He'll make it?

SIOBHAN
If he sleeps.

Mikhail rolls his shoulder, winces, tests the bandage. Rosalind sets down the IV and crosses to him, unwraps the dressing, checks the wound. He grimaces. Teddy turns the volume up, trying for another voice.

MIKHAIL
How is it?

ROSALIND
Clean. You'll keep the arm.

MIKHAIL
That is good. I like the arm.

ROSALIND
(dry)
So does your wife, I imagine.

MIKHAIL
She likes the other one more.

They laugh, quietly, careful of the sleepers. The laugh dies. The church holds its breath. Siobhan walks down the aisle between the cots, letting her hand trail along the pews.

RADIO (V.O.)
Dagger Six, copy that. Ceasefire at oh-six-hundred. Repeat, ceasefire at oh-six-hundred.

TEDDY
(whispering)
Ma'am. Ceasefire. Six a.m.

SIOBHAN
(not turning)
Thank you, Corporal.

Teddy pulls the headphones off. Rubs his face. Rosalind sits on the end of Mikhail's cot, suddenly unable to stand. Mikhail pats her shoulder with his good hand.

At the altar, Siobhan stops. Lifts the bloody sheet. Folds it, corner by corner. Lays it on the stone. Straightens it. Steps back.

Behind her, Oswin stirs, murmurs, and falls still again. The candle gutters and holds.

SIOBHAN (CONT'D)
(to the room)
Everyone rest. I'll take first watch.

Rosalind and Mikhail nod. Teddy lies back on his cot, the radio on his chest, fading into static and sleep. Siobhan sits in the front pew, hands folded, eyes on the candle.
